-- JKPSI 2017 — Testbook-style explanations for all 120 questions

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='An operating system (OS) manages computer hardware and software. Android, iOS, and Windows are all operating systems. Microsoft Office is a productivity application suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint), not an OS.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q1';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='All three statements about Particulate Matter (PM) are correct. PM is a mixture of solid particles and liquid droplets. Construction sites and unpaved roads are major sources of PM2.5 and PM10. Prolonged exposure causes respiratory and cardiovascular disorders — a major air quality concern.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q2';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='World Environment Day is observed every year on 5th June. It was established by the UN in 1972 following the Stockholm Conference on the Human Environment. The day promotes global awareness and action for the protection of the environment.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q3';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Paris Agreement (COP21, December 2015) aims to limit the rise in global average temperature to well below 2°C above pre-industrial levels and pursue efforts to limit the increase to 1.5°C. It does not ban hydrocarbons entirely, making option D incorrect.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q4';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Fourth Buddhist Council was held at Kundalvana, Kashmir, during the reign of Kushan Emperor Kanishka (1st–2nd century CE). It was presided over by Vasumitra and Asvaghosha. The Mahavibhasha, a comprehensive commentary on Abhidhamma, was compiled here.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q5';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Border Area Development Programme (BADP) is administered by the Ministry of Home Affairs. It aims to meet the special developmental needs of people living in remote and inaccessible areas along international borders, addressing infrastructure and social gaps.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q6';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Archaeological excavations at Ambaran-Pamberwan near Akhnoor (Jammu) revealed significant Buddhist remains, including stupas, monasteries, and artifacts. The site is considered one of the most important Buddhist archaeological sites in the Jammu region.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q7';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Wind energy is a renewable source because wind is continuously replenished by nature. Natural gas, petroleum, and nuclear energy (uranium) are non-renewable as they depend on finite resources. Renewable sources also include solar, hydroelectric, geothermal, and tidal energy.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q8';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='A light year is the distance light travels in one year in a vacuum, approximately 9.46 × 10¹² km. Despite having "year" in its name, it is a unit of distance, not time. It is used to measure astronomical distances between stars and galaxies.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q9';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The McMahon Line is a disputed boundary between India and China in the eastern Himalayas. It was agreed upon in the 1914 Simla Convention between British India and Tibet. China does not recognize this line and the dispute has been a source of tension, including the 1962 India-China War.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q10';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Annie Besant established the Home Rule League in India in 1916, modeled on the Irish Home Rule Movement. Ireland''s Home Rule movement, which sought self-governance within the British Empire, inspired similar demands in India. B.G. Tilak also founded a separate Home Rule League the same year.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q11';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Statements (i) and (ii) are correct. Tagore composed many patriotic songs during the Swadeshi movement and returned his knighthood in protest of the Jallianwala Bagh massacre (1919). Statement (iii) is incorrect — he accepted the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1913 for Gitanjali.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q12';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Francois Bernier, a French physician, spent 12 years in Mughal India (1656–1668) and served as physician to Emperor Aurangzeb and Prince Dara Shikoh. His book "Travels in the Mughal Empire" is a valuable historical source on Mughal society, administration, and economy.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q13';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Under the Government of India Act, 1935, the Governor was not removable by a vote of no confidence in the Provincial legislature. The Governor was appointed by and responsible to the Crown (British Government). This made the executive accountable to London, not the provincial assembly, which was a major limitation of the Act.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q14';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Statements (i) and (iii) are correct about India''s unorganized sector. It employs over 90% of the workforce (more than the organized sector) and workers are generally not organized into trade unions. Statement (ii) is incorrect — job security and work regulation are actually worse in the unorganized sector.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q15';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Competition Commission of India (CCI), established under the Competition Act, 2002, regulates mergers, acquisitions, and anti-competitive practices in India. SEBI regulates securities markets, while DIPP handles industrial policy. CCI ensures that mergers do not harm market competition.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q16';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='As per capita income rises, the share of food in total expenditure decreases — this is known as Engel''s Law. As incomes grow, consumers spend relatively more on non-food items (education, health, entertainment). The absolute amount spent on food may increase, but its share in total spending falls.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q17';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Wild Ass Sanctuary in the Rann of Kutch, Gujarat, is the only natural habitat of the Indian Wild Ass (Equus hemionus khur), locally called Ghudkhur. Established in 1972, it is the world''s largest Wild Ass sanctuary. The species is listed as Near Threatened on the IUCN Red List.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q18';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='PM Ujjwala Yojana was launched in May 2016 to provide free LPG connections to women from Below Poverty Line (BPL) households. It aimed to replace traditional cooking fuels (firewood, dung cakes) with clean LPG, reducing indoor air pollution and improving the health and dignity of women.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q19';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Odisha (specifically the Gahirmatha Beach and Rushikulya coast) is the largest nesting site for Olive Ridley turtles in the world, apart from Costa Rica. The state issues fishing bans during the nesting season (January–May) to protect these endangered sea turtles, which arrive in massive aggregations called "arribada".' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q20';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Megasthenes was a Greek ethnographer and ambassador sent by Seleucus I Nicator to the court of Chandragupta Maurya (322–297 BCE), the founder of the Maurya Empire. His work "Indica" is a primary source for Mauryan history, society, and administration.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q21';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Hampi, a UNESCO World Heritage Site in Karnataka, contains the ruins of the Vijayanagar Empire''s capital Vijayanagara. The empire flourished from the 14th to 17th centuries. The site includes temples, palaces, elephant stables, and the iconic Vittala Temple with its stone chariot.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q22';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='E.V. Ramasamy Periyar (E.V.R. Naicker), initially a Congress member and associate of Gandhi, later broke away and launched the Self-Respect Movement in 1925 in Tamil Nadu. The movement aimed at social equality, rationalism, and the eradication of caste discrimination. He founded the Dravidar Kazhagam party.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q23';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Non-Cooperation Movement (1920–22), launched by Gandhi, came to an abrupt end after the Chauri Chaura incident on 4 February 1922, where an angry mob set fire to a police station in Gorakhpur (UP), killing 22 policemen. Gandhi suspended the movement, believing non-violence had been compromised.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q24';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Mango shower refers to pre-monsoon rainfall that occurs in Kerala and Karnataka, which helps in the ripening of mangoes. It is also known as "Blossom Shower" in the context of coffee plantations. These are convectional rains that occur during the hot summer months (March–May) in these states.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q25';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Kaveri (Cauvery) is the most important river of Tamil Nadu, originating in Coorg, Karnataka. It is called the "Dakshina Ganga" (Ganges of the South). The river is crucial for irrigation, and its waters are shared between Karnataka and Tamil Nadu, often leading to disputes.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q26';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Wular Lake, the largest freshwater lake in India, was formed due to tectonic activity in the Kashmir Valley. It lies in the Kashmir Valley, Bandipora district. The lake is fed by the Jhelum River and serves as a vital wetland ecosystem, though it has shrunk significantly due to siltation and encroachment.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q27';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The duration of day and night varies with latitude. At the equator (0°), days and nights are roughly equal throughout the year. As latitude increases, seasonal variation in day length increases. At the poles, there are periods of continuous daylight (midnight sun) and continuous darkness. Longitude affects time zones, not day length.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q28';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Sri Lanka is separated from mainland India by the Palk Strait (between Tamil Nadu and northern Sri Lanka) and the Gulf of Mannar (between Tamil Nadu and southern Sri Lanka). Adam''s Bridge (Ram Setu) is a chain of limestone shoals connecting Pamban Island (India) to Mannar Island (Sri Lanka).' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q29';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The ideals of Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity in India''s Preamble were inspired by the French Revolution of 1789. The French motto "Liberté, Égalité, Fraternité" directly influenced Dr. B.R. Ambedkar, who studied in France. The American Revolution inspired fundamental rights, and the Soviet constitution influenced socialism in the Preamble.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q30';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Manas National Park and Tiger Reserve is located in Assam, at the foothills of the Himalayas, on the India-Bhutan border. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a Project Tiger Reserve. It is home to the endangered Assam roofed turtle, pygmy hog, Indian rhinoceros, and golden langur.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q31';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Platelets (thrombocytes) are small, colorless cell fragments in blood that play a crucial role in blood clotting (hemostasis). When a blood vessel is injured, platelets aggregate at the site and release chemicals that trigger the clotting cascade. RBCs transport oxygen/CO₂; WBCs provide immunity.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q32';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The loudness (intensity) of a sound wave is determined by its amplitude — the maximum displacement of particles from their rest position. Greater amplitude means more energy in the wave and louder sound. Frequency determines pitch (how high or low a sound is), while speed depends on the medium.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q33';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Francois Bernier, the French physician and philosopher, served as personal physician to Prince Dara Shikoh (eldest son of Emperor Shah Jahan) and later to Emperor Aurangzeb. His account provides valuable insights into the Mughal court, including the succession war between Aurangzeb and Dara Shikoh.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q34';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Under Article 352 (as amended by the 44th Amendment, 1978), a National Emergency can be proclaimed only during war, external aggression, or armed rebellion. The original phrase "internal disturbance" was replaced with the more specific "armed rebellion" to prevent misuse, as happened during the 1975 Emergency.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q35';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Article 32 grants the right to move the Supreme Court for enforcement of Fundamental Rights — a power that embodies judicial review. Dr. Ambedkar called Article 32 the "heart and soul" of the Constitution. Article 13 declares laws inconsistent with Fundamental Rights void, but Article 32 is the direct remedy.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q36';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The SAARC Secretariat is headquartered in Kathmandu, Nepal. Established in 1985, SAARC (South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation) has 8 member states: India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan, Sri Lanka, Maldives, and Afghanistan. The Secretariat was established in January 1987.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q37';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='PM Jan Dhan Yojana (PMJDY), launched on 28 August 2014, is the world''s largest financial inclusion program. It provides universal access to banking services, allowing every household to open a zero-balance bank account. It also provides a RuPay debit card, overdraft facility, and accident insurance cover.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q38';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Arunachal Pradesh is India''s easternmost state (longitude ~97°E), bordering China, Myanmar, and Bhutan. Gujarat is the westernmost state (longitude ~68°E), on the Arabian Sea coast. Rajasthan and Assam are incorrect as Rajasthan is west but not westernmost, and Assam is east but not easternmost.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q39';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Both statements are incorrect. (i) A Money Bill can only be introduced in the Lok Sabha, not in the Rajya Sabha. (ii) The determination of whether a Bill is a Money Bill is made by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, not the Union Cabinet. The Rajya Sabha can only make recommendations on Money Bills, which the Lok Sabha may accept or reject.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q40';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Government of India Act, 1919 (Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms) clearly demarcated the jurisdictions of the Central and Provincial Governments through a division of subjects. It introduced "Dyarchy" at the provincial level (dividing subjects into "Reserved" for the Governor and "Transferred" to elected ministers).' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q41';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Golan Heights is a strategic rocky plateau in the Middle East, captured by Israel from Syria during the 1967 Six-Day War. It remains a disputed territory. In 2019, the US recognized Israeli sovereignty over the Golan Heights — a move condemned by the UN and most countries as a violation of international law.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q42';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='NITI Aayog (National Institution for Transforming India) was established on 1 January 2015 to replace the Planning Commission (set up in 1950). Unlike the Planning Commission, NITI Aayog is a policy think-tank and advisory body without power to allocate funds. It acts as a platform for cooperative federalism.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q43';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Both statements are incorrect. (i) The executive power of the Union is vested in the President of India (Article 53), not the Prime Minister. (ii) The PM need not be a member of the Lok Sabha — they can be a member of the Rajya Sabha (e.g., H.D. Deve Gowda was a PM without being a member of either House initially). The PM must be a member of either House of Parliament.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q44';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Fifth and Sixth Schedules of the Indian Constitution contain provisions for the administration and protection of tribal (Scheduled Tribes) areas. The Fifth Schedule applies to Scheduled Areas in states like Jharkhand, Odisha, and Madhya Pradesh. The Sixth Schedule covers tribal areas in northeastern states, giving tribal councils autonomous legislative powers.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q45';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV, Articles 36–51) enshrine the idea of a Welfare State in India. They direct the State to promote the welfare of people by securing social, economic, and political justice. Key DPSPs include equal pay for equal work, free legal aid, and a uniform civil code.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q46';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Finland is known as the "Land of a Thousand Lakes" (actually over 188,000 lakes), making up about 10% of its total area. Its capital is Helsinki. The Netherlands is known as the "Land of Windmills and Tulips," Denmark for its Vikings heritage, and Poland for its plains and forests.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q47';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Nubra Valley Glacier is located in Ladakh (Union Territory), NOT in Arunachal Pradesh — making option A the incorrectly matched pair. Milam Glacier (Uttarakhand), Bhaga Valley Glacier (Himachal Pradesh), and Zemu Glacier (Sikkim) are all correctly matched. The Nubra Valley lies between the Karakoram and Ladakh ranges.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q48';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Central Potato Research Institute (CPRI) is located in Shimla, Himachal Pradesh. Established in 1949, it is the premier research institute in Asia for potato improvement. Shimla was chosen due to its cool climate, ideal for potato cultivation and research. CPRI develops disease-resistant and high-yield potato varieties.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q49';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Oxygen (O₂) is not a greenhouse gas. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit infrared radiation, warming the atmosphere. Major greenhouse gases include water vapour (H₂O), carbon dioxide (CO₂), methane (CH₄), nitrous oxide (N₂O), and ozone (O₃). Oxygen is transparent to both visible light and infrared radiation.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q50';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Temperature in Kelvin can never be negative (statement iii) and can be zero or a positive definite quantity (statement iv). The Kelvin scale starts at absolute zero (0 K = −273.15°C), the theoretical lowest temperature where all molecular motion stops. Unlike Celsius or Fahrenheit, Kelvin has no negative values.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q51';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Graphite conducts electricity due to its hexagonal multilayer structure. In each layer, carbon atoms form hexagonal rings with three covalent bonds — the fourth electron from each carbon is delocalized and free to move, allowing electrical conduction. This is unlike diamond, where all four electrons are covalently bonded, making it a non-conductor.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q52';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Nuclear Command Authority (NCA) is India''s apex body responsible for command, control, and operational decisions related to nuclear weapons. It was established in January 2003. The NCA has a Political Council (chaired by the PM) and an Executive Council (chaired by the National Security Advisor).' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q53';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Article 14 of the Indian Constitution guarantees equality before law and equal protection of laws to all persons (citizens and non-citizens) within the territory of India. It is based on the 14th Amendment of the US Constitution. Articles 12–35 form Part III (Fundamental Rights) of the Constitution.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q54';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Counter Insurgency and Jungle Warfare School (CIJWS) of the Indian Army is located at Vairengte in Mizoram (on the Mizoram-Assam border). It is one of the finest counter-insurgency training establishments in the world and trains soldiers in jungle warfare and counter-insurgency operations.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q55';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='INS Kalvari is the first Scorpene-class submarine of the Indian Navy, commissioned on 14 December 2017. Built at Mazagon Dock Shipbuilders Limited (MDL), Mumbai, under Project-75, it is named after a deep-sea tiger shark. It is capable of undertaking multifarious types of combat operations.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q56';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Doctrine of Lapse, implemented by Lord Dalhousie (1848–56), allowed the British East India Company to annex any princely state where the ruler died without a natural male heir. This expanded British territorial control significantly, annexing states like Satara, Jhansi, Nagpur, and Awadh, fueling resentment that contributed to the 1857 Revolt.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q57';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Permanent Settlement of 1793 (not 1799) introduced the Zamindari system in Bengal under Lord Cornwallis. Zamindars were recognized as permanent proprietors of land and were required to pay a fixed revenue to the British. This created a class of loyal landed aristocracy but impoverished peasants.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q58';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Only statement (i) is correct — there are exactly four Grand Slams annually. Statement (ii) is wrong: the first Grand Slam of the year is the Australian Open (January), not the US Open (August–September). Statement (iii) is wrong: both the Australian Open and US Open are played on hard courts, not clay. The French Open (Roland Garros) is played on clay.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q59';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Iron deficiency anaemia is treated with iron and folic acid supplements. Anaemia is a condition where the blood lacks adequate healthy red blood cells or haemoglobin. Osteoporosis is treated with calcium/Vitamin D, goitre with iodine, and protein-energy malnutrition with protein-rich diet.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q60';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Bernoulli''s principle states that an increase in the speed of a fluid occurs simultaneously with a decrease in pressure. It is based on the Law of Conservation of Energy — the total mechanical energy (kinetic + potential + pressure energy) of a flowing fluid remains constant. Applications include aircraft lift and carburettors.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q61';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Water at the bottom of a frozen lake is approximately 4°C because water has maximum density at 4°C. When the surface water cools below 4°C, it becomes less dense and stays on top (eventually freezing), while the denser 4°C water sinks to the bottom. This property, called anomalous expansion, protects aquatic life in freezing conditions.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q62';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Taungup Pass (also called Taungup Corridor) is a strategic mountain pass in the Arakan (Rakhine) region of Myanmar, connecting the Indian state of Mizoram with Myanmar. It serves as an important corridor for India-Myanmar connectivity under the India-Myanmar-Thailand Trilateral Highway project.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q63';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Among the given options, only Babar Kot (Gujarat, India) and Dholavira (Kutch, Gujarat, India) are IVC sites located in India. Mohenjo-daro and Harappa are located in present-day Pakistan. Dholavira is a UNESCO World Heritage Site known for its sophisticated water management. Babar Kot is known for burial structures and artifacts.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q64';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='1 atmosphere (atm) is defined as the pressure exerted by a 760 mm (76 cm) column of mercury at 0°C at sea level (standard gravity). This is equivalent to 101,325 Pa (pascals). A bar (100,000 Pa) is slightly less than 1 atm. Poise is a unit of dynamic viscosity, not pressure.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q65';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Insulin, a hormone produced by the beta cells of the pancreas, is essential for the uptake and utilization of glucose by body cells. Without insulin, glucose cannot enter cells and accumulates in the blood (hyperglycemia), leading to diabetes mellitus. GH is growth hormone; TSH is thyroid-stimulating hormone; Cortisol is a stress hormone.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q66';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Altitude sickness occurs at high altitudes because the partial pressure of oxygen decreases as altitude increases, even though the percentage of oxygen remains constant (~21%). This makes it harder for the body to absorb sufficient oxygen. Symptoms include headache, nausea, and fatigue. Acclimatization or supplemental oxygen is the remedy.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q67';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Statement A is incorrect — confessions CAN be used as evidence under certain conditions. Under Indian Evidence Act, a confession made to a magistrate (judicial confession) is admissible as evidence. However, a confession made to a police officer is generally not admissible. The statement is overly broad and hence incorrect.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q68';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the Pacific (ESCAP) is headquartered in Bangkok, Thailand. It is the largest UN regional development organization, serving 53 member states across Asia and the Pacific. It focuses on sustainable development, connectivity, and inclusive economic growth.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q69';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Habeas Corpus ("You shall have the body") is a writ issued by the Supreme Court or High Courts directing a person detaining another to produce the detainee before the court. It protects individuals from unlawful detention or imprisonment. Mandamus directs public authorities to perform duties; Certiorari quashes decisions of lower courts.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q70';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Jammu and Kashmir Rifles (JAK Rifles) is a light infantry regiment of the Indian Army. Raised in 1947, it played a pivotal role in the defence of Kashmir against Pakistani tribal forces. The regiment has earned numerous gallantry awards including the Param Vir Chakra. It should not be confused with the J&K Armed Police.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q71';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Services sector contributes approximately 55% to India''s GDP, making it the largest sector. It includes IT/ITES, banking, retail, hospitality, healthcare, and education. Agriculture contributes about 18% and Manufacturing (Industry) about 27%. India is a global leader in IT and business process outsourcing (BPO) services.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q72';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Simon Commission (1927) recommended replacing dyarchy with responsible government in the provinces. It proposed a federal constitution and bicameral legislatures in major provinces. It was boycotted across India (as it had no Indian members) with protests of "Simon Go Back." The Indian National Congress rejected its findings and responded with the Nehru Report (1928).' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q73';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='A fiscal stimulus involves government actions to boost aggregate demand during a recession. Cutting taxes (i) increases disposable income and consumer spending. Increasing government spending (ii) directly injects money into the economy. Abolishing subsidies (iii) reduces purchasing power and is contractionary — not a stimulus measure.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q74';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The circulatory (cardiovascular) system consists of blood vessels (arteries, veins, capillaries), the heart, and blood. The heart pumps blood through vessels to deliver oxygen and nutrients to cells and remove waste products. The lymphatic system (lymph vessels and nodes) is a separate but related system that supports immunity and fluid balance.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q75';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Alps and Himalayas are classic examples of fold mountains formed by the process of folding. Fold mountains are created when tectonic plates collide (convergent boundaries), causing the rock layers to buckle and fold upward. The Alps were formed by collision of the African and Eurasian plates; the Himalayas by the Indian and Eurasian plates.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q76';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The ozone layer (ozonosphere) is located in the stratosphere, approximately 15–35 km above Earth''s surface. It absorbs most of the Sun''s harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiation, protecting life on Earth. CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons) deplete the ozone layer, creating the "ozone hole" mainly over Antarctica.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q77';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Slate is a fine-grained metamorphic rock formed from shale or mudstone under low-grade metamorphic conditions (heat and pressure). Shale, limestone, and sandstone are sedimentary rocks formed by deposition and compaction of sediments. Metamorphic rocks are formed from existing rocks transformed by heat, pressure, or chemical processes.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q78';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Thermal (coal-based) power plants are the largest source of electricity in India, contributing about 70% of total electricity generation. Coal is the primary fuel, followed by natural gas and diesel. Hydropower is the second-largest source (~12%). India has been rapidly expanding solar and wind capacity in recent years.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q79';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Chandrashekhar Azad was the commander-in-chief of the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association (HSRA), formed in 1928 after reorganizing the Hindustan Republican Army. Azad, Bhagat Singh, and Sukhdev were key HSRA members involved in the Lahore Conspiracy Case and the Central Legislative Assembly bombing. Azad died on 27 February 1931, shooting himself to avoid arrest.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q80';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The enamel of teeth is the hardest substance in the human body, harder than bone. Tooth enamel is 96% hydroxyapatite (a crystalline calcium phosphate), making it extremely hard but also brittle. Once damaged, enamel cannot regenerate as it has no living cells after formation.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q81';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The temperate grasslands of North America are called Prairies. They stretch across the Great Plains of the USA and Canada. Other grasslands: Pampas (South America), Savanna/Veldt (Africa), Steppes (Central Asia/Eastern Europe), Downs (Australia), and Selvas refer to tropical rainforests of the Amazon.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q82';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Simlipal Biosphere Reserve is located in the Mayurbhanj district of Odisha. It is one of India''s largest national parks and a Project Tiger reserve. It is home to the rare melanistic (black) tigers and the world''s largest population of gaur. The name "Simlipal" comes from the Simul (silk cotton) trees that grow abundantly there.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q83';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Nitrogen (N₂) does not have well-known natural allotropes under normal conditions. Carbon has allotropes: diamond, graphite, fullerene, graphene. Oxygen has allotropes: O₂ (dioxygen) and O₃ (ozone). Sulphur has many allotropes including rhombic and monoclinic sulphur. Allotropes are different structural forms of the same element.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q84';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Statements (i) and (iii) are correct. Diamond is the hardest natural substance (10 on Mohs scale) while graphite is soft (1–2 on Mohs scale). Diamond is a bad conductor of electricity (all 4 electrons are bonded), while graphite is a good conductor (delocalized electrons). Both are allotropes of carbon with different crystal structures.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q85';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Thermal conductivity order is Steel > Water > Wood. Metals like steel have high thermal conductivity due to free electrons that transfer heat rapidly. Water has moderate conductivity. Wood is a poor conductor (insulator) due to air pockets in its cellular structure. This is why we use wooden handles on utensils and metal pans for cooking.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q86';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='A body weighs more at the poles than at the equator because: (1) Earth is slightly flattened at the poles, so the poles are closer to Earth''s center, increasing gravitational pull. (2) The centrifugal effect of Earth''s rotation is maximum at the equator, reducing effective gravity. Thus, 10 kg at the equator weighs slightly more than 10 kg at the poles.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q87';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='India Gate in New Delhi was originally named "All India War Memorial." It was designed by Sir Edwin Lutyens and inaugurated in 1931. It commemorates the 82,000 soldiers of the British Indian Army who died in World War I (1914–18) and the Third Anglo-Afghan War (1919). The Amar Jawan Jyoti (eternal flame) was added in 1972.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q88';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Statement (i) is correct — the Supreme Court in the Kesavananda Bharati case (1973) declared the Preamble to be an integral part of the Constitution. Statement (ii) is incorrect — "Secular" and "Socialist" were NOT in the original Preamble; they were added by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment in 1976 during the Emergency period under Indira Gandhi.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q89';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Vice President of India is elected by members of an Electoral College consisting of members of both Houses of Parliament (Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha) — by secret ballot. Unlike the President, members of State Legislative Assemblies do NOT participate in the VP election. The VP is elected under Article 66 of the Constitution.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q90';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Navroz (Nowruz) is the Persian New Year celebrated by the Parsi community (Zoroastrians) in India. It falls on the first day of spring (around March 21 as per the Persian calendar) or in August as per the Shahenshahi Parsi calendar used in India. "Navroz" means "New Day" in Persian.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q91';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The 100th anniversary of the Champaran Satyagraha was commemorated in 2017. The Champaran Satyagraha of 1917 was Gandhi''s first civil disobedience movement in India, launched against the forced indigo cultivation (tinkathia system) imposed by British planters in Bihar. Its success established Gandhi as a mass leader in India.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q92';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Parliament can normally legislate on subjects in the Union List (List I), which includes defence, foreign affairs, currency, banking, railways, etc. Parliament can also legislate on the Concurrent List (both Parliament and States). The State List is exclusively for state legislatures. There is no "Financial List" in the Constitution.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q93';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The series is 7, 13, 21, 31, 43. The differences between consecutive terms are: 13−7=6, 21−13=8, 31−21=10, 43−31=12. The differences increase by 2 each time, forming an arithmetic progression (6, 8, 10, 12...). So the missing number X = 7 + 6 = 13.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q94';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The product of Price × Consumption is constant: 40×60 = 50×48 = 60×40 = 75×32 = 2400. This shows an inverse relationship. At Rs. 80 per litre: Consumption = 2400 ÷ 80 = 30 litres. This is an example of an inverse proportion relationship between price and quantity demanded.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q95';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The candidate attempted 10 questions, securing 50% marks in each. Total marks scored = 10 × (50/100) × m = 5m (where m = marks per question). This equals 25% of total marks. Let total questions = n. Then 5m = 25% × n×m → 5 = n/4 → n = 20 questions.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q96';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Let son''s age = x. Then father = 9x and mother = 8x. Father + Mother = 9x + 8x = 17x = 68 → x = 4. So the son is 4 years old, father is 36 years, and mother is 32 years. Verification: 36 + 32 = 68 ✓' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q97';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Initial: A=100, B=100, C=100, D=100. After transactions: A pays 20 to B → A=80, B=120. B pays 10 to C → B=110, C=110. C gets 30 from D → C=140, D=70. Final: A=80, B=110, C=140, D=70. C has the most money (Rs. 140), making C the richest.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q98';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='After each 1 km, Ram turns left and Afzal turns right. Since they move in opposite directions, both end up turning in the same absolute direction after each km. They trace parallel square paths offset by 2 units, maintaining a constant perpendicular distance of 2 km. They are always 2 km apart — they never meet.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q99';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Let glass capacity = 12 units (LCM of 3 and 4). Glass 1: milk = 4, water = 8. Glass 2: milk = 3, water = 9. Mixed: total milk = 4+3 = 7, total water = 8+9 = 17. Ratio water:milk = 17:7. This is a standard mixture and alligation problem.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q100';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The uncovered side = 20 feet. Area = length × width = 680 sq. ft. Width = 680 ÷ 20 = 34 feet. Fencing required = two widths + one length (3 sides) = 34 + 34 + 20 = 88 feet.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q101';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Let the two parts be x and y. x + y = 700. Average = 350. Difference = (1/5) × 350 = 70. So x − y = 70 and x + y = 700. Solving: x = 385, y = 315. The smaller part = 315 hectares.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q102';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='At 8 am, Train P (from P, speed 20 km/h) has traveled 20 km. Remaining distance = 110 − 20 = 90 km. From 8 am, relative speed = 20 + 25 = 45 km/h. Time to meet = 90 ÷ 45 = 2 hours after 8 am = 10 a.m. Meeting point: 2 hours × 25 km/h = 50 km from Q.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q103';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Sales tax = 30 paise = Rs. 0.30. Tax rate = 6%. Taxable purchase amount = 0.30 ÷ 0.06 = Rs. 5. Total paid for taxable items = Rs. 5 + Rs. 0.30 = Rs. 5.30. The remaining Rs. 25 − Rs. 5.30 = Rs. 19.70 was for tax-free items.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q104';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The seller sells 40% and still has 420 oranges, meaning 420 represents the remaining 60% (100% − 40%). Original quantity = 420 ÷ 0.60 = 700 oranges. Verification: 40% of 700 = 280 sold, remaining = 420 ✓' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q105';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='From 8 a.m. to 2 p.m. = 6 hours. The hour hand completes one full rotation (360°) in 12 hours. So in 6 hours, it rotates = (6/12) × 360° = 180°. The hour hand moves at 0.5° per minute or 30° per hour.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q106';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='If the average of 20 non-zero numbers is zero, the sum of all numbers = 0. For this to happen with non-zero numbers, there must be at least one negative number. The maximum number of positive numbers = 19 (with 1 very large negative number to balance all 19 positives, making sum = 0).' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q107';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The ladder is 5 m long, foot is 3 m from the wall, angle of elevation = 45°. Using Pythagoras: height² = 5² − 3² = 25 − 9 = 16 → height = 4 m. Note: the problem states angle = 45° but the geometry (3-4-5 right triangle) gives angle = arctan(4/3) ≈ 53°. The answer from Pythagorean theorem is 4 m.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q108';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='P''s rate = 1/15 work/day. Q''s rate = 1/20 work/day. Together per day = 1/15 + 1/20 = 4/60 + 3/60 = 7/60. In 4 days = 4 × 7/60 = 28/60 = 7/15. Work remaining = 1 − 7/15 = 8/15.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q109';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Let son''s present age = s, father''s = f. s + f = 60. Six years ago: f − 6 = 5(s − 6) → f − 6 = 5s − 30 → f = 5s − 24. Substituting: s + 5s − 24 = 60 → 6s = 84 → s = 14 years. After 6 years, son''s age = 14 + 6 = 20 years.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q110';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The 2020 Summer Olympics were held in Tokyo, Japan (July 23 – August 8, 2021, delayed by one year due to the COVID-19 pandemic). Tokyo had previously hosted the Summer Olympics in 1964. India won 7 medals at Tokyo 2020, including Neeraj Chopra''s historic gold in javelin throw.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q111';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The Zoji-la Tunnel project (14.15 km) will be Asia''s longest bidirectional highway tunnel, connecting Baltal (Sonmarg) to Minamarg in the Kargil district of Ladakh. It will provide all-weather connectivity on the Srinagar-Leh National Highway, reducing the 3.5-hour crossing to 15 minutes and opening the route year-round.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q112';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Saikhom Mirabai Chanu is an Indian weightlifter from Manipur. In 2017, she became World Weightlifting Champion in the 48 kg category in Anaheim, USA. She won a Silver medal at the Tokyo 2020 Olympics in the 49 kg category — India''s first Olympic weightlifting medal since Karnam Malleswari''s bronze in 2000.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q113';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Mithali Raj (Mithali Dorai Raj) captained the Indian women''s cricket team in the ICC Women''s Cricket World Cup 2017 held in England. India reached the final but lost to England by 9 runs at Lord''s. She is India''s highest run-scorer in women''s international cricket and the first to score 10,000 ODI runs.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q114';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Kailash Satyarthi, founder of Bachpan Bachao Andolan (Save Childhood Movement, 1980), was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 2014 jointly with Malala Yousafzai. He has worked to free over 85,000 children from child labour and trafficking. The Nobel was awarded for his struggle against the suppression of children and for the right of all children to education.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q115';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The National Health Protection Scheme (now Ayushman Bharat – PM-JAY) provides health insurance cover of Rs. 5 lakh per family per year to approximately 10 crore poor and vulnerable families (50 crore beneficiaries). Announced in Union Budget 2018-19, it is the world''s largest government-funded health insurance scheme.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q116';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='The National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences (NIMHANS) is located in Bengaluru, Karnataka. It is a premier medical institution for mental health and neuroscience research, treatment, and education in India. NIMHANS was granted the status of Institute of National Importance in 2012.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q117';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Statement B is incorrect — Kalhana''s Rajtarangini was written in the 12th century (circa 1148–1150 CE), not the 15th century. It was commissioned by King Jayasimha Sahi of Kashmir. Written in Sanskrit verse, it chronicles the history of Kashmir from mythological times. It is considered one of India''s few genuine historical texts following a chronological method.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q118';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='All three statements about Bitcoin are correct. (i) Bitcoin is a cryptocurrency — a decentralized digital currency. (ii) It is the first decentralized digital currency, created by Satoshi Nakamoto in 2009. (iii) Bitcoin operates as a peer-to-peer network using blockchain technology — a distributed ledger that records all transactions publicly and immutably.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q119';

UPDATE vaultcore.questions SET explanation='Sultan Zain-ul-Abedin (1420–1470 CE) was the 8th Sultan of Kashmir and is widely regarded as the greatest ruler of the Shah Mir dynasty. Known as "Bud-shah" (Great King) by his people, he was famous for his religious tolerance, scholarly pursuits, and efforts to revive the Kashmiri language and culture. He invited Hindus who had fled Kashmir to return and abolished the hated cow-slaughter tax.' WHERE slug='jkpsi-2017-q120';
